  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 10,
    "lesson_title": "Rational Period Ratios and Periodicity",
    "lesson_core_move": "Classify a sum of periodic continuous-time signals as periodic or aperiodic by testing whether their fundamental periods have rational ratios and finding the least common period when they do.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Rational Period Ratios and Periodicity",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Rational Period Ratios and Periodicity",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A sum of periodic continuous-time signals is periodic only if every component comes back to the same state after one positive time shift. The cue is a shared repeat time: look for a single $T>0$ that works for every component.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+T)=x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the base case of two component fundamental periods, $T_1$ and $T_2$. If one period is an integer multiple of the other, they already share a repeat time. For example, periods of $2$ s and $6$ s line up again after $6$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{T_1}{T_2}\\in\\mathbb{Q}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In general, the periods must have a rational ratio. If $T_1/T_2=p/q$ in lowest terms, then multiplying through gives a common period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{T_1}{T_2}=\\frac{p}{q}\\quad(\\gcd(p,q)=1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "qT_1=pT_2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The least common period is the smallest positive shared multiple. For any finite sum of periodic components, find the smallest $T>0$ that equals an integer multiple of every component period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_{\\text{LC}}=\\min\\{T>0:\\,T=mT_1=nT_2,\\,m,n\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{>0}\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If the ratio is irrational, the components never line up exactly at any positive shift, so no finite common period exists and the sum is aperiodic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Rational Period Ratios and Periodicity",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Testing a Sum with an Integer Period Ratio",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A sum $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ has component fundamental periods $T_1=2$ s and $T_2=6$ s. Determine whether $x(t)$ is periodic and, if so, find a least common period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the rational-ratio cue from the tutorial: a sum of periodic signals is periodic when the component fundamental periods share a rational ratio. Here $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ with $T_1=2$ s and $T_2=6$ s, so the first check is the ratio.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{T_2}{T_1}=\\frac{6}{2}=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The ratio is an integer, so the periods are commensurate. Because $6=3\\cdot 2$, the larger period is already a shared repeat time for both components.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_{\\text{LC}}=6\\text{ s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $x(t)$ is periodic, and its least common period is $6$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Rational Period Ratios and Periodicity",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Finding a Least Common Period from a Noninteger Rational Ratio",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A sum $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ has component fundamental periods $T_1=2$ s and $T_2=3$ s. Determine whether $x(t)$ is periodic and, if so, find a least common period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the rational-ratio cue from the tutorial and the previous example: a sum is periodic when the component fundamental periods have a rational ratio. Here $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ with $T_1=2$ s and $T_2=3$ s, so the ratio is rational but not an integer.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{T_2}{T_1}=\\frac{3}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Even though neither period is an integer multiple of the other, the periods are commensurate. Find integers $m$ and $n$ with $mT_1=nT_2$; here $3T_1=2T_2=6$, so both components repeat after $6$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_{\\text{LC}}=6\\text{ s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $x(t)$ is periodic, and its least common period is $6$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Rational Period Ratios and Periodicity",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Classifying a Sum with an Irrational Period Ratio",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A sum $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ has component fundamental periods $T_1=2$ s and $T_2=2\\sqrt{2}$ s. Determine whether $x(t)$ is periodic.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the rational-ratio test again. Here $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)$ with $T_1=2$ s and $T_2=2\\sqrt{2}$ s, so first check the ratio of the component periods.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{T_2}{T_1}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational, the periods are not commensurate. That means there are no positive integers $m$ and $n$ with $mT_1=nT_2$, so the sum has no common repeat time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $x(t)$ is aperiodic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 10,
        "lesson_title": "Rational Period Ratios and Periodicity",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Finding the Least Common Period for Multiple Periodic Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A sum $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)+x_3(t)$ has component fundamental periods $T_1=2$ s, $T_2=3$ s, and $T_3=4$ s. Determine whether $x(t)$ is periodic and, if so, find a least common period.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same rational-ratio test, but now all three component periods must fit one shared repeat time. Here $x(t)=x_1(t)+x_2(t)+x_3(t)$ with $T_1=2$ s, $T_2=3$ s, and $T_3=4$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{T_2}{T_1}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\quad \\frac{T_3}{T_1}=2, \\quad \\frac{T_3}{T_2}=\\frac{4}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Each ratio is rational, so the three periods are commensurate. Look for a single time that is a multiple of all three periods. The smallest one is $12$ s, since $12=6T_1=4T_2=3T_3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T_{\\text{LC}}=12\\text{ s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, $x(t)$ is periodic, and its least common period is $12$ s.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
